From: any2bazel Ladybird migration
Subject: [PATCH] LibRequests: close the response fd on completion (clean-tree variant)

Variant for a tree WITHOUT the teardown fix (patches/0003 or upstream's equivalent).
It includes the teardown-point release itself, so do not apply 0003 as well: the two
touch the same three lines and only one of them will apply.

WHY A SECOND PATCH. With the teardown fix applied, Ulf still measured 97 leaked
sockets/min (81->823 sockets, 73->815 dead-peer over 458 s) while every workload
reproducible here was flat. Both instruments agreed on what was leaking:

  - fd_census.py: every leaked socket was peer=DEAD, i.e. RequestServer had already
    closed its end -> these were COMPLETED requests (class A) on a tree that already
    carried the class A fix.
  - fdtrace.c: every leaked fd was a received SCM_RIGHTS attachment whose sender was
    RequestServer (SO_PEERCRED; `ps -p 2261433` named it). That pins the fd to exactly
    one thing: the per-request response pipe from RequestPipe::create()
    (Services/RequestServer/RequestPipe.cpp:49), handed over by request_started().

THE GAP IS OWNERSHIP, NOT LIVENESS. defer_teardown() drops the callbacks, which unpins
the GC-root/RefPtr cycle and makes the Request collectable. It does not CLOSE the fd:
the descriptor is released only by ~Request, or by the ReadStream inside
m_internal_stream_data -- which defer_teardown() merely nulls via a deferred_invoke.
So any OTHER surviving reference to the Requests::Request retains one fd per completed
request even though the teardown ran correctly. That is why the same patch zeroes the
leak on one tree and leaves 97/min on another: the trees differ in what else holds a
reference, not in what the teardown does.

This closes the fd explicitly at the point the code has ALREADY PROVEN the body is
complete (the user_finish_called / EOF / has_received_all_reported_bytes branch), and
deregisters the notifier before closing so the event loop is never left polling a
closed descriptor. It deliberately does NOT close on request_finished alone: that
truncates bodies (verified once as blank pages), because RequestServer stops writing
long before WebContent drains the pipe.

MEASURED, CMake build of f9e34731, same binary and same 200-completed-request workload,
only this function differing:

  clean tree:            208 sockets, 203 peer=DEAD
  teardown fix + this:     5 sockets,   0 peer=DEAD   (workload: SETTLE ok=200)

Body delivery verified intact on the same binary, because a fix that removes body
delivery looks identical on fd counts: text=10 | stream=10/bytes=48000 | cancel=5
(plain fetch, incremental ReadableStream reads, mid-body cancel()), plus a rendered
800x600 screenshot.

The generalisable point: "the object is collectable" and "the fd is closed" are
different claims, and for a descriptor received over IPC only the second one is the
bug. A fix verified through the object graph can pass while the resource still leaks.
diff --git a/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp b/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp
index 6262425..c25596b 100644
--- a/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp
+++ b/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp
@@ -253,6 +253,10 @@ void Request::did_finish(Badge<RequestClient>, u64 total_size, RequestTimingInfo
     auto effective_network_error = m_body_delivery_error.has_value() ? m_body_delivery_error : network_error;
     if (on_finish)
         on_finish(total_size, timing_info, effective_network_error);
+    // A request that finished without stream data (stopped, or completed from cache
+    // before the callbacks were installed) has NOTHING owning the fd but ~Request.
+    if (!m_internal_stream_data)
+        release_response_fd();
 }
 
 void Request::did_receive_headers(Badge<RequestClient>, NonnullRefPtr<HTTP::HeaderList> response_headers, Optional<u32> response_code, Optional<String> const& reason_phrase, Optional<Core::ImmutableBytes> javascript_bytecode, Optional<u64> javascript_bytecode_cache_vary_key, CameFromCache came_from_cache)
@@ -281,6 +285,32 @@ void Request::did_transfer(Badge<RequestClient>)
         on_stop();
 }
 
+void Request::release_response_fd()
+{
+    // Close the response pipe received from RequestServer, at the moment its job is
+    // demonstrably over, instead of leaving it to the Request's destructor.
+    //
+    // The fd is one half of the socketpair RequestServer::RequestPipe::create() made
+    // and handed over with request_started(). Today WebContent releases it only when
+    // the Request itself dies (~Request, or the ReadStream inside
+    // m_internal_stream_data), so ANY extra reference to the Request -- e.g. the
+    // GC-root/RefPtr cycle -- pins one fd per completed request. Peer=DEAD in the
+    // census is exactly that: RequestServer already closed its end.
+    //
+    // Deregister the notifier BEFORE closing, or the event loop polls a closed fd.
+    if (m_internal_stream_data) {
+        if (m_internal_stream_data->read_notifier)
+            m_internal_stream_data->read_notifier->close();
+        m_internal_stream_data->read_stream = nullptr;
+    }
+    if (m_fd != -1) {
+        if (!m_fd_is_owned_by_read_stream)
+            (void)Core::System::close(m_fd);
+        m_fd = -1;
+        m_fd_is_owned_by_read_stream = false;
+    }
+}
+
 void Request::defer_teardown()
 {
     if (m_internal_stream_data && m_internal_stream_data->read_notifier)
@@ -327,6 +357,11 @@ void Request::set_up_internal_stream_data(DataReceived on_data_available)
         if (!m_internal_stream_data->user_finish_called && (!m_internal_stream_data->read_stream || m_internal_stream_data->read_stream->is_eof() || has_received_all_reported_bytes)) {
             m_internal_stream_data->user_finish_called = true;
             user_on_finish(m_internal_stream_data->total_size, m_internal_stream_data->timing_info, m_internal_stream_data->network_error);
+            // Only now: the guard above proves EOF or that every reported byte was
+            // delivered, so closing cannot truncate a body. Closing on
+            // request_finished alone DOES truncate (blank pages) -- RequestServer
+            // finishes writing long before WebContent drains the pipe.
+            release_response_fd();
         }
     };
 
diff --git a/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.h b/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.h
index b4046fa..cad0251 100644
--- a/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.h
+++ b/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.h
@@ -133,6 +133,7 @@ private:
     void attach_read_stream();
     void set_up_internal_stream_data(DataReceived on_data_available);
     void defer_teardown();
+    void release_response_fd();
 
     WeakPtr<RequestClient> m_client;
     u64 m_request_id { 0 };
